    "q-6294": {
      "dom_id": "step-q6294",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2a+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2a+1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
