    "q-118683": {
      "dom_id": "step-q118683",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we substitute $u=2x-3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x-3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[1-\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
