    "q-152780": {
      "dom_id": "step-q152780",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3t-2)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}t.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we substitute $u=3t-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3t-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3t-2)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3t-2)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-3/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{(a)^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{(4)^{3/2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
